1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,899
Your app logs in to the database with one account.

2
00:00:03,899 --> 00:00:06,074
If that account is too powerful,

3
00:00:06,074 --> 00:00:06,618
one bug,

4
00:00:06,618 --> 00:00:08,182
or one stolen password,

5
00:00:08,182 --> 00:00:09,609
can erase everything.

6
00:00:10,159 --> 00:00:16,095
Today you take a database account that is a superuser with a weak password and no password check,

7
00:00:16,095 --> 00:00:17,135
and make it safe.

8
00:00:17,685 --> 00:00:19,616
Three fixes: a strong password,

9
00:00:19,616 --> 00:00:21,796
only the permissions the app needs,

10
00:00:21,796 --> 00:00:23,851
and a door that asks for the key.

11
00:00:24,401 --> 00:00:25,958
Give the app the least it needs.

12
00:00:27,508 --> 00:00:30,580
Here is what to expect, from the first minute to the last.

13
00:00:31,130 --> 00:00:34,735
One: the words. Role, privilege, grant, and least privilege.

14
00:00:35,285 --> 00:00:37,652
Two: a live demo on real post gress.

15
00:00:37,652 --> 00:00:39,296
We see the problem first,

16
00:00:39,296 --> 00:00:42,517
with the app account logging in with no password.

17
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:48,102
Three: we demote the account, set a strong password, and grant only what the app needs.

18
00:00:48,652 --> 00:00:52,472
Four: we close the door in the host based authentication file,

19
00:00:52,472 --> 00:00:53,273
and prove it.

20
00:00:53,273 --> 00:00:54,197
Then your turn,

21
00:00:54,197 --> 00:00:56,908
with a lab that judges you with five checks.

22
00:00:57,458 --> 00:00:59,975
This is lesson two of the databases domain.

23
00:01:00,525 --> 00:01:02,083
Give the app the least it needs.

24
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:06,650
Six ideas for safe database access.

25
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:08,551
A role is an account.

26
00:01:08,551 --> 00:01:09,451
It can log in,

27
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:10,159
own things,

28
00:01:10,159 --> 00:01:11,509
and hold permissions.

29
00:01:11,509 --> 00:01:14,468
A superuser role skips every permission check.

30
00:01:14,468 --> 00:01:16,011
Never give it to an app.

31
00:01:16,561 --> 00:01:19,338
A privilege is one permission on one object,

32
00:01:19,338 --> 00:01:20,916
like select on one table.

33
00:01:20,916 --> 00:01:22,368
You give it with grant,

34
00:01:22,368 --> 00:01:24,198
and take it back with revoke.

35
00:01:24,748 --> 00:01:28,972
Least privilege means the app gets only what it needs to do its job,

36
00:01:28,972 --> 00:01:29,780
nothing more.

37
00:01:29,780 --> 00:01:31,830
If it only reads and adds orders,

38
00:01:31,830 --> 00:01:33,196
it cannot delete them.

39
00:01:33,746 --> 00:01:35,842
A serial column uses a sequence.

40
00:01:35,842 --> 00:01:40,034
An insert needs permission on the sequence as well as the table.

41
00:01:40,034 --> 00:01:41,213
A common surprise.

42
00:01:41,763 --> 00:01:44,766
The P G H B A file decides who may connect,

43
00:01:44,766 --> 00:01:45,534
from where,

44
00:01:45,534 --> 00:01:47,070
and how they prove it.

45
00:01:47,070 --> 00:01:48,537
Trust means no proof.

46
00:01:48,537 --> 00:01:50,143
Scram means a password.

47
00:01:50,143 --> 00:01:53,006
Always use scram for network connections.

48
00:01:53,556 --> 00:01:56,753
Secrets such as passwords live outside the code,

49
00:01:56,753 --> 00:01:59,685
in an environment file or a secrets manager,

50
00:01:59,685 --> 00:02:01,150
never in a repository.

51
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:08,012
Go to the lab folder.

52
00:02:09,562 --> 00:02:11,461
Look around. Type: L S.

53
00:02:12,011 --> 00:02:14,001
A data folder and a server log.

54
00:02:14,001 --> 00:02:15,991
A post gress server is running,

55
00:02:15,991 --> 00:02:18,688
and an app account called shop app exists.

56
00:02:19,238 --> 00:02:39,526
Log in as the app, no password.

57
00:02:40,076 --> 00:02:44,577
It worked. Nobody asked for a password. Anyone on this machine can be the app.

58
00:02:45,127 --> 00:03:03,197
Who are the roles?

59
00:03:03,747 --> 00:03:04,398
Two roles,

60
00:03:04,398 --> 00:03:05,961
and both are superusers.

61
00:03:05,961 --> 00:03:07,980
The app account is a superuser.

62
00:03:07,980 --> 00:03:10,650
A superuser skips every permission check.

63
00:03:10,650 --> 00:03:12,408
That is far too much power.

64
00:03:12,958 --> 00:03:24,990
Count the superusers.

65
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:29,807
Two. Write this down before you change anything. It is your first lab answer.

66
00:03:30,357 --> 00:03:35,839
Write the answer.

67
00:03:36,389 --> 00:03:41,744
Open psql as admin.

68
00:03:42,294 --> 00:03:58,081
Demote the app.

69
00:03:58,631 --> 00:04:01,489
Alter role. The app is now an ordinary role.

70
00:04:02,039 --> 00:04:17,869
Set a strong password.

71
00:04:18,419 --> 00:04:21,235
Done. The old password, change me, no longer works.

72
00:04:21,785 --> 00:04:45,038
Grant only what the app needs.

73
00:04:45,588 --> 00:04:47,443
Read customers and products.

74
00:04:47,443 --> 00:04:48,768
Read and add orders.

75
00:04:48,768 --> 00:04:49,895
And the sequence,

76
00:04:49,895 --> 00:04:51,617
because an insert uses it.

77
00:04:51,617 --> 00:04:52,479
Nothing else.

78
00:04:53,029 --> 00:04:55,866
Leave psql. Type: backslash, Q.

79
00:04:56,416 --> 00:05:12,907
Save the password for the app.

80
00:05:13,457 --> 00:05:16,102
Read it back. Type: cat, app dot env.

81
00:05:16,652 --> 00:05:22,028
In real life, this file is never committed to a repository, and only the app may read it.

82
00:05:22,578 --> 00:05:31,005
Open the access file.

83
00:05:31,555 --> 00:05:32,247
Two lines.

84
00:05:32,247 --> 00:05:35,641
Local connections through the socket are trusted.

85
00:05:35,641 --> 00:05:37,580
The network line says trust.

86
00:05:37,580 --> 00:05:39,104
Change trust to scram,

87
00:05:39,104 --> 00:05:39,451
shaw,

88
00:05:39,451 --> 00:05:41,251
two fifty-six on line two.

89
00:05:41,251 --> 00:05:41,944
Then save.

90
00:05:42,494 --> 00:05:53,289
Reload the config.

91
00:05:53,839 --> 00:05:56,463
True. The change is live, without a restart.

92
00:05:57,013 --> 00:06:19,711
Try with no password.

93
00:06:20,261 --> 00:06:23,376
No password supplied. The door asks for the key now.

94
00:06:23,926 --> 00:06:46,134
Log in with the password.

95
00:06:46,684 --> 00:06:48,668
Three orders. The app can read.

96
00:06:49,218 --> 00:06:54,103
Try to delete.

97
00:06:54,653 --> 00:06:56,968
Permission denied for table orders.

98
00:06:56,968 --> 00:06:59,547
The app can no longer erase the orders,

99
00:06:59,547 --> 00:07:00,605
even by mistake.

100
00:07:01,155 --> 00:07:06,531
Try to create a table.

101
00:07:07,081 --> 00:07:10,708
Permission denied for schema public. Least privilege, working.

102
00:07:11,258 --> 00:07:13,626
Run the judge.

103
00:07:14,176 --> 00:07:18,869
Five checks. Counted, demoted, password, door, and grants. Five out of five.

104
00:07:19,419 --> 00:07:21,382
Clean up.

105
00:07:21,932 --> 00:07:24,599
The server is stopped and everything is removed.

106
00:07:25,149 --> 00:07:34,877
Is it gone?

107
00:07:35,427 --> 00:07:37,944
No response. Nothing is left behind.

108
00:07:39,494 --> 00:07:41,862
Your task. Make the shop app account safe.

109
00:07:42,412 --> 00:07:43,949
One: count the superusers,

110
00:07:43,949 --> 00:07:46,491
and write the number into answers dot text,

111
00:07:46,491 --> 00:07:48,087
before you change anything.

112
00:07:48,637 --> 00:07:54,141
Two: demote the app account, and give it a new strong password, saved in app dot env.

113
00:07:54,691 --> 00:07:57,912
Three: grant only select on customers and products,

114
00:07:57,912 --> 00:07:59,933
and select and insert on orders,

115
00:07:59,933 --> 00:08:01,069
with the sequence.

116
00:08:01,619 --> 00:08:04,710
Four: change trust to scram in the access file,

117
00:08:04,710 --> 00:08:05,433
and reload.

118
00:08:05,433 --> 00:08:06,880
Five: test as the app,

119
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:07,867
then lab check,

120
00:08:07,867 --> 00:08:08,787
then lab down.

121
00:08:10,837 --> 00:08:14,613
Here is where to find everything. Pause on this screen, and write it down.

122
00:08:15,163 --> 00:08:16,811
The lab lives on GitHub,

123
00:08:16,811 --> 00:08:17,978
at it dash waves,

124
00:08:17,978 --> 00:08:19,558
slash dev ops dash A I,

125
00:08:19,558 --> 00:08:21,068
in the folder academy,

126
00:08:21,068 --> 00:08:21,755
slash lab.

127
00:08:22,305 --> 00:08:28,108
Two files: lab one twenty-seven dot S H, and shortcuts one twenty-seven dot S H.

128
00:08:28,658 --> 00:08:29,564
Install it,

129
00:08:29,564 --> 00:08:32,447
on any Linux machine: sudo install,

130
00:08:32,447 --> 00:08:34,342
dash m seven five five,

131
00:08:34,342 --> 00:08:36,730
lab one twenty-seven dot S H,

132
00:08:36,730 --> 00:08:37,966
into slash usr,

133
00:08:37,966 --> 00:08:38,955
slash local,

134
00:08:38,955 --> 00:08:39,943
slash S bin.

135
00:08:40,493 --> 00:08:45,363
Load the short names with: source shortcuts one twenty-seven dot S H.

136
00:08:45,363 --> 00:08:46,563
You get two: lab,

137
00:08:46,563 --> 00:08:47,128
and P G.

138
00:08:47,678 --> 00:08:49,329
You need post gress Q L,

139
00:08:49,329 --> 00:08:50,567
server and client,

140
00:08:50,567 --> 00:08:51,255
installed,

141
00:08:51,255 --> 00:08:53,800
and port fifty-four seventy-one free.

142
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,551
The lab runs its own private post gress,

143
00:08:56,551 --> 00:08:57,790
in its own folder.

144
00:08:58,340 --> 00:09:00,047
Lab up prepares everything.

145
00:09:00,047 --> 00:09:01,754
Lab check judges your work.

146
00:09:01,754 --> 00:09:03,271
Lab down removes it all.

147
00:09:03,271 --> 00:09:05,294
Only run it on your own machine.

148
00:09:07,344 --> 00:09:09,413
Pass or fail. You need all five.

149
00:09:09,963 --> 00:09:12,075
One: you counted the superusers first.

150
00:09:12,625 --> 00:09:14,908
Two: the app is no longer a superuser.

151
00:09:15,458 --> 00:09:18,999
Three: a strong new password, saved in app dot env.

152
00:09:19,549 --> 00:09:22,109
Four: the network login demands a password.

153
00:09:22,659 --> 00:09:26,286
Five: the app can read and insert orders, and nothing more.

154
00:09:26,836 --> 00:09:28,137
To pass, all five.

155
00:09:30,187 --> 00:09:31,958
Your turn, on your own machine.

156
00:09:32,508 --> 00:09:35,580
One: lab up, count the superusers, and write the answer.

157
00:09:36,130 --> 00:09:40,503
Two: demote the app, and set a new password, saved in app dot env.

158
00:09:41,053 --> 00:09:44,488
Three: grant only what the app needs, including the sequence.

159
00:09:45,038 --> 00:09:49,411
Four: change trust to scram, reload, and test with and without a password.

160
00:09:49,961 --> 00:09:53,780
Five: lab check until you see five out of five. Then lab down.

161
00:09:54,330 --> 00:09:56,164
Pause the video, and begin now.

162
00:10:11,714 --> 00:10:12,589
Quick check.

163
00:10:13,139 --> 00:10:15,827
Why must an app account never be a superuser?

164
00:10:16,377 --> 00:10:20,025
What does least privilege mean for an app that reads and adds orders?

165
00:10:20,575 --> 00:10:23,028
Why did the insert need a grant on the sequence?

166
00:10:23,578 --> 00:10:27,247
What is the difference between trust and scram in the access file?

167
00:10:27,797 --> 00:10:30,149
Objectives covered: M C fourteen,

168
